id=sssp
label=SSSP (Standard Solid-State Pseudopotentials)
families=paw us nc
provenance=external
generator=curated-selection
upstream=Materials Cloud
author=Prandini, Marrazzo, Castelli, Mounet, Marzari
reference=https://doi.org/10.24435/materialscloud:f3-ym
license=see per-element source library
summary=Not a generator but a per-element *selection* of the best-performing pseudopotential from GBRV, PSlibrary, SG15, PseudoDojo, Wentzcovitch and Goedecker, together with recommended ecutwfc/ecutrho.
notes=SSSP does not make pseudopotentials and publishes no generator: it publishes a verdict. For each element it names which existing library won a large convergence and equation-of-state verification against all-electron reference data, and the cut-off that winner needs. PseudoForge bundles the four 1.3.0 selection tables (120 KB) and resolves each element to the profile that actually owns it -- 87 of the 103 are libraries this tool generates locally, so an SSSP run is mostly a generated one. The 60 MB pseudopotential archives are deliberately not bundled: shipping them would be shipping copies of other people's libraries under SSSP's name. The recommended cut-offs are the one thing here that cannot be regenerated -- they are the output of the verification campaign, not of any atomic code -- and they are carried into every run that uses this profile. Run 'pseudoforge list sssp' to see the whole table.
cutoff_table=sssp-efficiency.csv
engine=ld1
available=103 elements, PBE and PBEsol, efficiency and precision. Selection tables bundled; 87 of 103 generatable here.
xc_available=pbe pbesol
rel_available=scalar
families_note=whichever family the chosen library uses, per element
formats=upf xml
